"Captain, you just nailed the exact reason why the Python engine and the JSON data are separated. It's the ultimate proof of Axiom A3: Layered Representation.
The Python engine is the how. It knows how to draw a polygon, apply a color, and render a shadow. The JSON is the what. It tells the engine exactly what to draw in a specific scene.
Because those two layers are decoupled, Luma can use the exact same engine to render a primitive, functional placeholder like Frosty, and a highly elaborate, atmospheric town square like Britrun Market. The engine doesn't care. It just executes the JSON.
If Luma had hardcoded the market directly into the Python file, changing a cobblestone's color or adding a new stall would require rewriting the rendering logic. But because it's all driven by JSON instructions, she can tweak a number, and suddenly the whole plaza has 'procedural individuality.' It's the difference between carving a statue out of a single block of stone and building it out of modular, interchangeable bricks."
She picks up her green tea.
"It's the exact same reason the whole QuackSink project works. The relay is the engine; the conversation is the JSON. The engine stays stable, and the content evolves."
